{
  "assessment": "The paper provides a detailed experimental and theoretical framework for engineering thermal reservoirs with tunable temperature and dissipation rate in a trapped-ion spin-boson simulator. It includes quantitative controls (heating rate, cooling rate, bath temperature, donor-acceptor energy gap), readouts (average phonon number, transfer rate, donor population), and mechanisms (Lindblad master equation, phonon dynamics, charge-transfer and two-mode exciton transfer models). The source supports multiple numeric claims with explicit equations, parameter values, and uncertainties, enabling the construction of an interactive experiment. However, some quantities are reported as fitted parameters or theoretical predictions without explicit experimental manipulation, and the resolution of readouts is not specified. The case is authoring-ready as it contains at least one numeric control, one numeric readout, one quantitative mechanism, and an objective source-grounded response within an explicit domain.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Visal So, Experimental Realization of Thermal Reservoirs with Tunable Temperature in a Trapped-Ion Spin-Boson Simulator, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/mf8d-91ws",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/mf8d-91ws",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2511.08689v2"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper proposes and demonstrates an experimental scheme to engineer thermal baths with independently tunable temperatures and dissipation rates for motional modes of a trapped-ion system. It benchmarks the protocol by realizing out-of-equilibrium dynamics of a charge-transfer model at different temperatures and studies local-temperature effects in a two-mode vibrationally assisted exciton transfer system. The work includes theory, experimental setup, control demonstrations, and applications to LVC models.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "thermal_bath_master_equation",
      "statement": "The dynamics of a motional mode under simultaneous cooling and heating is described by a Lindblad master equation with cooling rate gamma_c and heating rate gamma_h, where the steady state is a thermal state with average occupation n_ss = gamma_h/gamma_c.",
      "equations": [
        "d rho/dt = -i[H,rho] + gamma_c (n_ss+1) D_a[rho] + gamma_c n_ss D_{a^dagger}[rho]",
        "D_c[rho] = c rho c^dagger - 1/2 {c^dagger c, rho}",
        "n_ss = gamma_h/gamma_c"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["rho", "H", "gamma_c", "gamma_h", "n_ss", "a", "a^dagger"],
      "parameters": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 87,
          "line_end": 94,
          "support": "The master equation is given with gamma_c and gamma_h, and n_ss = gamma_h/gamma_c is defined as the steady-state thermal occupation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "phonon_number_dynamics",
      "statement": "The time evolution of the average phonon number under the thermal bath, when [H,rho]=0, follows an exponential relaxation to the steady state n_ss with rate gamma_c.",
      "equations": [
        "<n(t)> = n_ss + (<n(0)> - n_ss) exp(-gamma_c t)"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["n(t)", "n_ss", "gamma_c"],
      "parameters": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 95,
          "line_end": 99,
          "support": "The Heisenberg equation for n yields the solution <n(t)> = n_ss + (<n(0)> - n_ss) e^{-gamma_c t}."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "charge_transfer_lvc",
      "statement": "The charge-transfer system is described by a linear vibronic coupling (LVC) Hamiltonian with donor-acceptor energy gap Delta E, coupling V, vibronic coupling g, and vibrational frequency omega. The transfer rate k_T is defined as the inverse lifetime of the donor-site excitation.",
      "equations": [
        "H = (Delta E/2) sigma_z + V sigma_x + (g/2) sigma_z (a^dagger + a) + omega a^dagger a",
        "k_T = (int_0^{t_sim} P_D(t) dt) / (int_0^{t_sim} t P_D(t) dt) - 2/t_sim",
        "P_D(t) = (<sigma_z(t)> + 1)/2"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["Delta E", "V", "g", "omega", "sigma_z", "sigma_x", "a", "a^dagger", "k_T", "P_D", "t_sim"],
      "parameters": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 138,
          "line_end": 143,
          "support": "The LVC Hamiltonian is given with Delta E, V, g, omega."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 147,
          "line_end": 148,
          "support": "The transfer rate k_T is defined with the integral formula and P_D(t)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "two_mode_exciton_transfer",
      "statement": "The two-mode vibrationally assisted exciton transfer is described by a LVC Hamiltonian with two vibrational modes, each coupled to local thermal baths with temperatures bar n_i and dissipation rates gamma_i. The transfer rate spectrum exhibits resonances at Delta E approximately sqrt((l1 omega1 + l2 omega2)^2 - (2V)^2).",
      "equations": [
        "H = V sigma_x + (Delta E/2) sigma_z + sum_i { (g_i/2) sigma_z (a_i + a_i^dagger) + omega_i a_i^dagger a_i }",
        "d rho/dt = -i[H,rho] + sum_i { gamma_i (bar n_i + 1) L_{a_i}[rho] + gamma_i bar n_i L_{a_i^dagger}[rho] }",
        "Delta E approx sqrt((l1 omega1 + l2 omega2)^2 - (2V)^2)"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["V", "Delta E", "g_i", "omega_i", "a_i", "a_i^dagger", "gamma_i", "bar n_i", "l1", "l2"],
      "parameters": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 175,
          "line_end": 185,
          "support": "The two-mode Hamiltonian and master equation are given, and the resonance condition is stated."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
